BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-ID:gcal_4h7dspldpuviiv4o1slj9o5p5c
X-SOURCE:CITA Seminars
DTSTART:20261109T200000Z
DTEND:20261109T210000Z
DTSTAMP:20261107T055651Z
UID:0tt6jlqp8hm50pih8clteihjpc@google.com
CREATED:20260825T182032Z
DESCRIPTION:<p>Title: <b>From Resolved ISM Physics to Galaxy Formation Mode
 ls: Star Formation\, Winds\, and Cosmic Rays</b></p>\n\n<p>Abstract: Galaxy
  formation simulations cannot resolve the interstellar medium (ISM)\, where
  stars form and feedback acts\, so\nstar formation and feedback enter them 
 through subgrid models that are traditionally based on the local gas densit
 y\nand calibrated empirically. In this talk\, I will show how we use high-f
 idelity simulations of the multiphase ISM to\nbridge this gap. The TIGRESS 
 frameworks model kiloparsec-scale patches of galactic disks with resolved s
 upernova\nremnants\, and now include UV radiation transfer\, non-equilibriu
 m photochemistry\, and cosmic-ray transport with\nself-consistent scatterin
 g rates. From these simulations we identify robust physical principles and 
 turn them into\nsubgrid models. First\, in the pressure-regulated\, feedbac
 k-modulated (PRFM) theory\, the star formation rate follows\nthe weight of 
 the ISM that feedback must support. The resulting star formation–pressure r
 elation agrees with\nobservations of nearby galaxies\, and implemented in g
 alaxy formation simulations\, it predicts star formation rates\nthat differ
  from density-based recipes. Second\, galactic winds are multiphase: cool g
 as carries most of the mass and\nhot gas most of the energy\, and subgrid w
 ind models built on this picture regulate galaxy growth preventively\,\nthr
 ough the circumgalactic medium. Third\, cosmic rays leave star formation ra
 tes and wind launching unchanged but\naccelerate cool gas above the disk\, 
 producing more mass-loaded outflows. Finally\, I will introduce LIT (Learni
 ng the\nISM with TIGRESS)\, a simulation suite across galactic environments
  designed to connect ISM simulations with\nobservations.</p>\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20261105T155747Z
LOCATION:1318A
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Chang Goo Kim (Princeton University)
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
