X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:Sanko Speaks
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261121T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261121T164500
UID:6aa2f9b53dd190003e7105a1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:A free\, culturally affirming drop-in program for adults that 
 promotes mental wellness through creative activities\, discussion\, and he
 althy coping strategies. Guided by the concept of Sankofa\, participants e
 xplore connections between past experiences and present wellbeing while bu
 ilding skills for managing stress and supporting mental health. Activities
  vary based on community interests and needs.\nThis program is in collabor
 ation with\nTAIBU Community Health Centre\n.
LOCATION:Kennedy/Eglinton\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aa2f9b53dd190003e7105a1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ad796bae06996003a84bf4a"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:Go-Between Girl: Author Andrea Gunraj on Identity\, Memory\, and t
 he Indo-Caribbean Woman Experience in Canada
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261121T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261121T150000
UID:6ad796bae06996003a84bf4a@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Meet\nAndrea Gunraj\n\, acclaimed essayist and author of\nGo
 -between Girl: My Indentured Roots as Reclaimed Present\n\, The Lost Siste
 r and The Sudden Disappearance of Seetha. Based out of Toronto\, Gunraj wr
 ites about underseen stories and connections. She will discuss her latest 
 work\, Go-Between Girl\, through storytelling and personal reflection\, ex
 ploring themes of family\, migration\, belonging\, and the complex interse
 ctions of Caribbean\, Indian\, and Canadian identities.\nGunraj will be in
  conversation with\nVictoria Kuketz\n\, a public policy leader\, journalis
 t (IG @victoriakuketz)\, global social inclusion leader\, and 2026 Obama F
 oundation Scholar. The discussion will be followed by an audience Q&A and 
 book signing.\nPresented by the\nCaribbean Canadian Indian Cultural Collec
 tive (CCICC)\nand\nIndo Caribbean Educators Network (ICEN)\n\, this event 
 offers a unique opportunity to engage with one of the most compelling voic
 es in contemporary Indo-Caribbean literature. Attendees will gain insight 
 into Gunraj's personal journey as a professional civic leader and Indo-Car
 ibbean woman\, and how historical and cultural experiences have shaped Ind
 o-Caribbean people. Discover the power of storytelling as memory\, communi
 ty building\, and cross-cultural understanding.\nWhether you are familiar 
 with Andrea's work or discovering it for the first time\, this event promi
 ses an inspiring and thought-provoking evening celebrating literature\, he
 ritage\, and the diverse narratives that enrich Canada's multicultural lan
 dscape.\nFree program. Please register. Doors open at 12:45 pm. At 1:00 p
 m\, seating becomes first-come\, first-served until capacity is reached.\n
 Organization Info:\nThe\nCanadian Caribbean Indian Cultural Collective (CC
 ICC)\nis a community organization representing Indians (currently referred
  to as South Asians) of various ethnicities\, regions\, and faiths\, who a
 re the descendants of the indentured labourers taken by the British to the
 ir colonies in the Caribbean and around the world. Their mission is to sha
 re their stories of struggle\, resilience and contributions across the Car
 ibbean and here in Canada.\nPlace a hold or borrow from Toronto Public Lib
 rary:\nGo-between Girl: My Indentured Roots as Reclaimed Present\n(book)\n
 Go-Between Girl\n(ebook)\nGo-Between Girl\n(e-audiobook)\nThe Lost Sister\
 n(book)\nOther\nbooks and formats available by Andrea Gunraj via TPL\n.\n
 Information Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is c
 ollected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, se
 ctions 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for ev
 ent and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about ho
 w the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff
  at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4
 W 2G8.
LOCATION:Palmerston\, Theatre
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ad796bae06996003a84bf4a
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
