BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ac7e7bbea50a10050a6a31f"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:Tiny Titans: Insects Up Close | Book Launch & Panel Talk with Auth
 or & Photographer Dara Ojo
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261202T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261202T200000
UID:6ac7e7bbea50a10050a6a31f@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Discover the hidden world of arthropods through stunning macro
  photography — a visual journey celebrating their beauty\, complexity\, 
 and essential role in our ecosystems.\nMeet\nDara Ojo\n\, an award-winning
  macro photographer and conservation storyteller whose work brings the hid
 den world of arthropods into vivid relief. Ojo's book\,\nTiny Titans : The
  Silent Keepers of Our Ecosystem\nreveals the fascinating lives of insects
 \, spiders\, and other small creatures that quietly sustain life on Earth.
  Captured across Canada\, Mexico\, Costa Rica\, and beyond\, Dara Ojo’s 
 photography highlights the intricate details and diversity of these often-
 overlooked species.\nOjo will be leading the conversation with:\nAntonia 
 Guidotti\n\,\na Collections Technician with a focus on Insects & Arachnid
 s at the Royal Ontario Museum.\nLorraine Johnson\n\, author and native pl
 ant and ecological gardening advocate\, bridging the connection between in
 sects\, plants\, habitat and practical conservation action.\nDr. Sandra Re
 han\n\, professor at York University and principal investigator of the Re
 han Lab with a focus on wild bees\, pollinator biology and conservation.
  Rehan's research explores the biology\, behaviour and evolution of bees\,
  with a particular interest in understanding and protecting native pollina
 tors.\nDr. Laura Timms\n\, Senior Specialist of Natural Heritage Manageme
 nt at Credit Valley Conservation and Adjunct Professor with the Daniels 
 Faculty’s Institute of Forestry and Conservation.\nJoin in this evening 
 to celebrate macro photography and the intricate beauty and ecological imp
 ortance of insects and spiders\, creatures that quietly anchor global biod
 iversity\, pollination\, and climate resilience.\nFree program. Register f
 or a reminder.\n***Doors open at 5:30 pm. At 6:00 pm\, seating becomes fir
 st-come\, first-served until capacity is reached.***\n5:30 pm:\nDoors open
 . Mingle and chat with fellow nature and environmental enthusiasts!\n6:00
  pm:\nPresentation begins. Enjoy a highly visual presentation combining p
 hotography\, science\, conservation and storytelling\, taking the audience
  behind the scenes of six years documenting tiny worlds across five contin
 ents and exploring why these overlooked species matter. A light\, inform
 al conversation with guest speakers will explore why arthropods are so es
 sential to our ecosystems\, with a discussion of challenges impacting loca
 l species\, and how science\, photography and art can work together to hel
 p people notice\, understand and care about the natural world. An audience
  Q&A follows this discussion.\n7:30 pm:\nBook signing with books available
  for purchase.\n***\nThis program is part of TPL's\nOur Fragile Planet\npr
 ogram series\, supported by Friends of Toronto Public Library\, South Chap
 ter.​\nQuestions? Please contact North York Central Library - Business\,
  Science & Technology Department.\n***\nPlace a hold or borrow from Toront
 o Public Library:\nTiny Titans : The Silent Keepers of Our Ecosystem\n(boo
 k) - Dara Ojo\nA Garden for the Rusty-patched Bumblebee\n(book) - Lorraine
  Johnson\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on
  this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Lib
 raries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only
  be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Que
 stions about how the library handles your information can be directed to A
 nswerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, T
 oronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Room 101 - 1st floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ac7e7bbea50a10050a6a31f
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
