BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Philosophy": "https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/colloquium-olivia-bailey-uc-berkeley/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:philosophy
X-SOURCE:UofT Philosophy
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261203T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261203T170000
DTSTAMP:20261103T165637Z
CREATED:20260827T202552Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261103T165637Z
UID:35592-1796310000-1796317200@philosophy.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:Colloquium (Olivia Bailey\, UC Berkeley)
DESCRIPTION:The Department is pleased to welcome Olivia Bailey\, an assistant professor in the Department of Philosophy\, at UC Berkeley for a colloquium on December 3\, 2026. She works on questions in moral psychology and the history of moral philosophy (particularly the British moralists). Her current research is concerned with the ethical and epistemic significance of empathy. \nTitle\nThe See-Through Heart: Transparency\, Rationality\, and Emotional Self-Knowledge \nAbstract\nHow can I arrive at an answer to the question of how I feel? I could study my behavior\, or defer to my psychoanalyst. But it is hard to resist the idea that there is another way that I—and I alone—can speak to the question of what I feel\, one that does not rely on such indirect observational routes. One potentially powerful lead\, here\, is the fact that I can ordinarily expect my thinking about my feelings to make a difference to what I feel. Perhaps it is no coincidence that I have both a special way to answer the question of how I feel and a special power to shape my feelings through thought. \nI consider two established ways of developing this lead\, an interpretationist view and a transparency view\, and draw out problems for both I then look to the phenomenology of emotional experience for help developing a hybrid of the two that avoids both sets of problems. On this account\, my (and yet special) ordinary authority to say what my emotion is hinges on my power to ‘make up my heart’ by settling a question about the relationship between my inner state and the world.
URL:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/colloquium-olivia-bailey-uc-berkeley/
LOCATION:Jackman Humanities Building 100
CATEGORIES:Graduate,St. George
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/Olivia-Bailey-325-x-225.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events": "https://events.yorku.ca/events/virtual-drop-in-session-master-of-biotechnology-management-mbm-graduate-diploma-in-biotechnology-gdb-3/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events
SUMMARY:Virtual Drop-In Session: Master of Biotechnology Management (MBM) 
 & Graduate Diploma in Biotechnology (GDB)
DTSTART:20261203T153000Z
DTEND:20261203T160000Z
DTSTAMP:20260721T141200Z
UID:MEC-cd6c652e6f498d4363bff3c4b63bd685@events.yorku.ca__20261203
SEQUENCE:4
CATEGORIES:Faculty of Graduate Studies,Graduate Diploma in Biotechnology,G
 raduate Program in Biotechnology Management
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20260721
DESCRIPTION:The Master of Biotechnology Management  is a course- and 
 experiential learning-based graduate program designed for students who 
 hold a bachelor of science degree in biology\, biological sciences\, bio
 technology\, life science\, or related discipline\, and have little/no wor
 k experience in biotechnology. It provides practical interdisciplinary tra
 ining in biotechnology and management to enhance student competitiveness
  and employability in the biotechnology sector. Graduates from this prog
 ram are prepared to contribute to the sector in roles that require scient
 ific\, technical\, and management skills and competencies.  \nThe Grad
 uate Diploma in Biotechnology is a graduate certification for students w
 ho hold a bachelor of science degree in biology\, biological sciences\, 
 biotechnology\, life science\, or related discipline\, and have little/no 
 work experience in biotechnology. It provides scientific and technical 
 training in biotechnology to enhance graduate competitiveness and emplo
 yability in the biotechnology sector. Students acquire the skills and c
 ompetencies required to address labour gaps in the biotechnology sector
  and strengthen the Canadian economy. \nDrop by one of our virtual sessio
 ns on Thursdays from 11:30 a.m. to 12:00 p.m. to meet the graduate pr
 ogram director and graduate program administrator who will be able to answ
 er questions about the programs. \nRegister in advance for this meeting:\
 nMeeting Registration – Zoom\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20260930
PRIORITY:5
TRANSP:OPAQUE
URL:https://events.yorku.ca/events/virtual-drop-in-session-master-of-biote
 chnology-management-mbm-graduate-diploma-in-biotechnology-gdb-3/
X-SOURCE:York University
END:VEVENT
