BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre": "https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/12/sedai-youth-creator-lab-showcase"}
X-SOURCE-ID:jccc
SUMMARY:Sedai Youth Creator Lab Showcase
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261216T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261216T213000
UID:02cddd5572f007fe354aa03e452c8249@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:For the past 2 years\, the JCCC has been working on enhancing 
 the accessibility of our Sedai Oral History Collection through long term p
 reservation\, outreach workshops on Oral History techniques and through ac
 tivation of our existing interviews. The capstone project of this Japanese
  Canadian Legacies Society funded project is our Sedai Youth Creator Lab. 
 Six youth have been working with three mentors and JCCC staff\, to collect
  and edit oral histories in 3 amazing short documentaries. Join us on Dece
 mber 16\, 2026 as we screen these documentaries for you for the first time
 . The evening will include 3 short documentaries and a panel discussion wi
 th our Youth and Mentors. A celebratory Reception will be held after the p
 anel discussion. This evening event will be a celebration of both the work
  of our Sedai Youth Creator Lab and our larger Sedai Project as we wrap up
  this amazing grant work. We hope that you are able to join us as we celeb
 rate this amazing project. When: December 16\, 2026 Doors Open: 6:00pm Scr
 eenings will begin promptly at 7:00pm Where: Kobayashi Hall at the Japanes
 e Canadian Cultural Centre Registration is highly recommended. Register\n\
 nCategories: Community Event\, Heritage
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/12/sedai-youth-creator-lab-showcase
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a5f86437b79214226aa1377"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:Northern District Book Club—Evenings
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261216T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261216T200000
UID:6a5f86437b79214226aa1377@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us for a friendly\, spirited discussion of a different bo
 ok on the third Wednesday of each month. We read an eclectic mix of mostly
  contemporary fiction.\nThere is also an afternoon session to discuss the 
 same book on the second Wednesday of each month at 2:00 p.m.  You are we
 lcome at either or both!\nSeptember 16\, 2026:\nOrbital\nby Samantha Harve
 y\nOctober 21\, 2026:\nPerfection\nby Lorenzo Patronico\nNovember 18\, 202
 6:\nStone Yard Devotional\nby Charlotte Wood\nDecember 16\, 2026:\nMaybe Y
 ou Should Talk to Someone\nby Lori Gottlieb
LOCATION:Northern District\, Main Floor Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a5f86437b79214226aa1377
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a8705bbdb86e4004d867404"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:The Delicious and Surprising History of The Dining Room: Author Ta
 lk with John Ota
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261216T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261216T200000
UID:6a8705bbdb86e4004d867404@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:John Ota presents​ and discusses his book\nThe Dining Room\n
 .\nExplore the stories behind twelve iconic dining rooms in homes around t
 he world—from a Japanese samurai house to Claude Monet's brilliant yello
 w dining room to Frida Kahlo's Casa Azul to Jackie Kennedy’s White House
 —and travel through time in pursuit of the ultimate design.​\nThe Dini
 ng Room is an enlightening and joyous celebration of history\, culture and
  the art of dining. Presenter John Ota's charming observations of the surp
 rising impact of dining rooms on our lives and the cherished moments we sh
 are within them will delight armchair travelers\, history fanatics and int
 erior design enthusiasts alike.\nJohn Ota has been involved with architect
 ure and design since 1978\, with degrees from Columbia University\, the Un
 iversity of Toronto and the University of British Columbia. His bestsellin
 g book\nThe Kitchen\nwas a 2021 Taste Canada Awards finalist and a 2021 Pa
 ris Gourmand Cookbook finalist in the book design category. After working 
 in architecture in Toronto\, New York\, and Vancouver\, and as lead on mul
 tiple Ontario Ministry of Culture projects\, John now loves to travel the 
 world visiting historic houses\, cooking regional food\, and meeting peopl
 e of all backgrounds.
LOCATION:Deer Park\, Room 204
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a8705bbdb86e4004d867404
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
