BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ae4e5379dbc2b002c922fa5"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:SP Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20270128T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20270128T150000
UID:6ae4e5379dbc2b002c922fa5@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Discuss diverse and inclusive titles from a variety of genres 
 on the fourth Thursday of every month!\nThis month we will be discussing\n
 Half of a Yellow Sun\nChimamanda Ngozi Adichie\nA profoundly gripping sto
 ry told primarily through the eyes and lives of Ugwu\, a 13 year old peasa
 nt houseboy who survives conscription into the raggedy Biafran army\, and 
 twin sisters Olanna and Kainene\, who are from a wealthy and well connecte
 d family.\nRegistration is required and begins December 28\, 2026. Copies 
 Available in branch after registration.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nT
 he personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Li
 brary under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d).
  The information will only be used for event and program registration\, de
 livery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your inform
 ation can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-39
 7-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Spadina Road\, Center of Room at Canoe Table
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ae4e5379dbc2b002c922fa5
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Older Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c980df81b67dfe32afc3a2"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_older_adults
SUMMARY:Resilience Reads: a climate neighbourhood reading party
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20270128T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20270128T153000
UID:69c980df81b67dfe32afc3a2@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us to share the joy of reading\, while building climate r
 esilience knowledge. Members are invited to discuss a book they are readin
 g\, tell stories\,  and empower each other to explore community-driven sol
 utions. Each party will start with a quiet read and reflect time\, with op
 portunities to join group discussions later on over refreshments.\nAUGUST\
 n– Fossil Fuel Folly\nAmerican War\nby Omar El Akkad or your favourite d
 ystopian read!\nor your favourite book featuring water!\nSEPTEMBER\n– We
  Need Pollinators\nThe History of Bees\nby Maja Lunde or your favourite an
 imal-centred book!\nOCTOBER\n– Climate Change is Scary\nThe Annual Migra
 tion of Clouds\nby Premee Mohamed or a different book that shares a scary 
 future ecological crisis!\nNOVEMBER\n- Climate Changes Everything\nMinistr
 y for the Future\nby Kim Stanley Robinson or your favourite time travel re
 ad!\nCopies are available in the branch to pick up\, while supplies last.\
 nRegistration is encouraged but drop ins are welcome!\nInformation Collect
 ion Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Tor
 onto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c
 )\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program reg
 istration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handl
 es your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl
 .ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:College/Shaw\, Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c980df81b67dfe32afc3a2
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events": "https://events.yorku.ca/events/virtual-drop-in-session-master-of-biotechnology-management-mbm-graduate-diploma-in-biotechnology-gdb-3/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events
SUMMARY:Virtual Drop-In Session: Master of Biotechnology Management (MBM) 
 & Graduate Diploma in Biotechnology (GDB)
DTSTART:20270128T153000Z
DTEND:20270128T160000Z
DTSTAMP:20260721T141200Z
UID:MEC-cd6c652e6f498d4363bff3c4b63bd685@events.yorku.ca__20270128
SEQUENCE:4
CATEGORIES:Faculty of Graduate Studies,Graduate Diploma in Biotechnology,G
 raduate Program in Biotechnology Management
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20260721
DESCRIPTION:The Master of Biotechnology Management  is a course- and 
 experiential learning-based graduate program designed for students who 
 hold a bachelor of science degree in biology\, biological sciences\, bio
 technology\, life science\, or related discipline\, and have little/no wor
 k experience in biotechnology. It provides practical interdisciplinary tra
 ining in biotechnology and management to enhance student competitiveness
  and employability in the biotechnology sector. Graduates from this prog
 ram are prepared to contribute to the sector in roles that require scient
 ific\, technical\, and management skills and competencies.  \nThe Grad
 uate Diploma in Biotechnology is a graduate certification for students w
 ho hold a bachelor of science degree in biology\, biological sciences\, 
 biotechnology\, life science\, or related discipline\, and have little/no 
 work experience in biotechnology. It provides scientific and technical 
 training in biotechnology to enhance graduate competitiveness and emplo
 yability in the biotechnology sector. Students acquire the skills and c
 ompetencies required to address labour gaps in the biotechnology sector
  and strengthen the Canadian economy. \nDrop by one of our virtual sessio
 ns on Thursdays from 11:30 a.m. to 12:00 p.m. to meet the graduate pr
 ogram director and graduate program administrator who will be able to answ
 er questions about the programs. \nRegister in advance for this meeting:\
 nMeeting Registration – Zoom\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20260930
PRIORITY:5
TRANSP:OPAQUE
URL:https://events.yorku.ca/events/virtual-drop-in-session-master-of-biote
 chnology-management-mbm-graduate-diploma-in-biotechnology-gdb-3/
X-SOURCE:York University
END:VEVENT
